      "At some point in nearly every growing company, someone finally scrolls to the bottom of the cloud bill and reacts with alarm. Cloud spending has a way of creeping upward quietly — a resource here, an oversized instance there, a forgotten environment humming along — until the total is genuinely startling. The encouraging news is that a large share of a typical cloud bill is pure waste rather than value, which means you can usually cut it substantially without giving up anything you actually need. The savings are sitting in plain sight once you look.",
      "This is a guide to trimming your cloud costs without cutting corners that matter.",
      "## Why cloud bills balloon",
      "Cloud spending grows quietly because the cloud makes it so frictionless to create resources and so easy to forget about them. Someone spins up a server for a test and never shuts it down. Resources are provisioned oversized 'to be safe' and never right-sized. An old environment keeps running long after it's needed. Each individual decision is small and reasonable, but they accumulate, and because nobody sees the running total in the moment, the waste compounds unnoticed until the bill forces a reckoning. The cloud's convenience is exactly what makes it leak money.",
