      "A few categories account for most overspending, and they're worth hunting down specifically:",
      "- **Oversized resources** — instances and services provisioned far larger than their actual usage, paid for in full regardless.\n- **Idle and forgotten resources** — servers, environments, and storage that are running but doing nothing, often left over from past projects.\n- **Inefficient usage patterns** — paying premium on-demand rates for steady, predictable workloads that could be committed at a discount.\n- **Data transfer and storage sprawl** — the quiet costs of moving and keeping data that accumulate out of sight.",
      "## The highest-leverage moves",
      "You don't need exotic techniques to make a real dent — just the discipline to do the obvious things:",
      "1. **Right-size everything.** Match resources to actual usage instead of guessed-at worst cases. Most oversized resources can shrink substantially with zero impact.\n2. **Turn off what you're not using.** Idle resources, forgotten environments, and non-production systems that don't need to run around the clock. This alone often recovers a surprising amount.\n3. **Commit to steady workloads.** For predictable, always-on usage, committed pricing is dramatically cheaper than on-demand. Paying on-demand rates for steady load is one of the most common and expensive mistakes.\n4. **Get visibility.** You can't manage what you can't see — understanding where the money actually goes is the prerequisite for cutting it, and often reveals surprises.",
