      "Ask users what they want from a product and almost nobody says 'speed' — and yet nothing drives them away faster than a slow one. Performance is the feature users never ask for and always feel: when it's good, it's invisible, and when it's bad, it colors everything. A slow product feels broken, frustrating, and untrustworthy, no matter how good its features are, because users experience the waiting before they experience anything else. Speed isn't a technical concern separate from the user experience — it *is* a core part of the experience, and treating it as an afterthought is a quiet mistake.",
      "This is a guide to why performance is a feature worth investing in and how to think about it.",
      "## Why speed is experience",
      "Here's the thing about performance: users don't experience your features directly, they experience them *through* the time it takes to use them. A brilliant feature behind a slow interaction feels like a chore. A simple feature that responds instantly feels delightful. Speed shapes the entire emotional texture of using a product — fast feels competent, responsive, respectful of the user's time; slow feels broken, careless, frustrating. Because waiting is the first thing users feel, performance frames how they perceive everything else. It's not separate from experience; it's the medium experience happens through.",
      "> Nobody asks for performance, and everybody feels it. Speed is the invisible feature that makes every other feature feel good or bad before the user has even used it.",
      "## The cost of slow",
      "Slowness carries real, measurable costs. Users abandon slow products — there's a well-documented relationship between delays and people simply leaving before they ever experience what you built. Those who stay enjoy it less, engage less, and trust it less, because slowness reads as a signal that the whole product is low-quality or unreliable. And the frustration compounds: every slow interaction is a small tax on the user's patience, and enough of them drive people to a faster alternative. Slow doesn't just annoy users; it quietly loses them, often before they've given the product a real chance.",
      "## Perceived speed matters as much as real speed",
      "A crucial and underused insight: how fast a product *feels* can matter as much as how fast it actually is. The same wait feels very different depending on how it's handled — showing immediate feedback that something is happening, displaying content progressively as it becomes available, and responding instantly to the user's action even while work continues in the background all make a product feel fast regardless of raw speed. Users perceive responsiveness, not milliseconds. Making a product *feel* fast — through immediate feedback and progressive loading — is often as valuable as making it technically faster, and sometimes easier.",
      "## Build performance in, protect it over time",
